  "notes": "First Q-simple, geometrically split example of [2,2,8] for the census (the gsplit cell is empty). Found by querying the LMFDB genus 2 data (devmirror.lmfdb.xyz, g2c_curves_new: is_simple_base = true, is_simple_geom = false, torsion_subgroup = [2,2,8]; geom_end_alg = Q x Q, end_alg = Q; smallest conductor among the curves of this group that the census verifier certifies). LMFDB conductor 9900. Checked locally with the census's own pipeline/magma/verify_lib.m (Magma V2.30-1): J(Q)_tors = [2,2,8], class gsplit, Q-simplicity prime 47 (chi = T^4 - 34*T^2 + 2209), splitness certificate: richelot over Q (a chain of two (2,2)-isogenies over Q reaches a Weil restriction of an elliptic curve E over Q(sqrt(5)) (the surface is E x E^sigma)). Submitted by F. Najman; the curve is due to the database cited under Reference. Rigorous conductor: J is (2,2)-isogenous over Q to Res_(K/Q) E with K the quadratic field of discriminant 5, so N(J) = |d_K|^2 * Norm(f_E) = 25 * 396 = 9900 (computed with Magma; conductor is an isogeny invariant).",
